From 8c6f3da65c0edd598be3429bf8a5d11e68fa9153 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave <dhuntlinux@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:10:28 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Search breadth-first and report matches as they are found

Recursive search descended into each subdirectory on the spot, and handed a
folder's matches over only once the whole subtree beneath it had been walked.
On a slow location the two together mean nothing appears for a very long time:
the crawl disappears into the first subtree it meets, and even the matches
sitting in the folder the user started from are withheld until everything
below them is done.

Measured against an SMB share of ~1.4 TiB over gigabit ethernet, searching for
"QCAD" from the share root: 0 results after 45 seconds. Nautilus, which queues
directories breadth-first and flushes hits every 500 entries, had 12 of them on
screen within 5 seconds of the same search.

Queue the subdirectories instead of recursing into them, and commit each
folder's matches as soon as that folder has been read (and every
THUNAR_SEARCH_COMMIT_BATCH entries within a large one). Same work, same
attributes, same number of round trips -- only the order changes, and results
now arrive while the crawl is still going rather than at the end of it. The
same search now shows 16 results after 5 seconds and 106 after 45.

Also delay the start of a changed search by THUNAR_SEARCH_START_DELAY. Every
character typed into the search entry emits "changed", which replaces the
running job, so a four-letter query used to start and abandon four crawls, each
one losing everything the last had found. Nautilus has the same guard in
CREATE_THREAD_DELAY_MS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
